DESCRIPTION:Sparse attention methods promise more efficient inference and e
 xtended context capabilities for Transformer-based Large Language Models (L
 LMs)\, yet their practical trade-offs and scaling behaviours are not fully 
 understood. In this talk\, I present the largest empirical evaluation of tr
 aining-free sparse attention methods to date\, examining how model size\, s
 equence length\, and sparsity level impact their performance across diverse
  long-sequence tasks. I highlight key insights\, including when sparse atte
 ntion is preferable over dense models\, limitations in consistently preserv
 ing performance across tasks\, and introduce novel scaling laws to predict 
 sparse attention performance beyond tested configurations.\n\nPiotr Nawrot 
 is a third-year PhD student at the University of Edinburgh advised by Edoar
 do M. Ponti and Ivan Titov. His research focuses on improving the efficienc
 y of Transformer models. More specifically\, he is interested in teaching t
 he models to dynamically allocate compute based on input difficulty\, which
  could pave the way for tokenization-free architectures and more compute-op
 timal models.\n\n-::~:~::~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:
 ~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~::~:~::-\nJoin with Google Meet: https://meet.google.c
 om/wdk-yipf-zjd\nOr dial: (US) +1 678-653-5936 PIN: 753754849#\nMore phone 
 numbers: https://tel.meet/wdk-yipf-zjd?pin=8294970755415&hs=7\n\nLearn more
  about Meet at: https://support.google.com/a/users/answer/9282720\n\nPlease
  do not edit this section.\n-::~:~::~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~
 :~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~::~:~::-
