DESCRIPTION:The WeightWatcher project is a long-standing research project t
 o provide both a theoretical basis for Why Deep Learning Works\, as well as
  a practical open-source tool for Deep Learning researchers and engineers t
 o develop better AI models. In this talk\, we will review the theoretical u
 nderpinnings of the theory behind this popular tool (currently over 175K do
 wnloads)\, including both the Theory of Heavy Tailed Self Regularization (H
 TSR\, published in 2021)\,--a phenomenology based on Heavy Tailed Random Ma
 trix Theory--and the more recently developed SemiEmpirical Theory of Learni
 ng (presented at NeurIPS 2023) which derives the conditions for ideal learn
 ing of a neural network (NN).  Most notably\, we will argue that the layers
  of a NN\, when properly optimized\, converge to state analogous to taking 
 1 step of the Exact WIlson Renormalization Group\, exhibiting both scale in
 variance and a universal critical exponent\, both which can be measured emp
 irically  (and without needing access to the test or training data!) and\, 
 most importantly\, be used to both gauge and improve  the performance of pr
 oduction NNs.\n\nCharles H. Martin earned his PhD in theoretical chemistry 
 from the University of Chicago under the mentorship of Professor Karl Freed
 —a group notable for producing Nobel laureates such as Bawendi (2023) and J
 umper (2024). He then completed an NSF Postdoctoral Fellowship at UIUC (wit
 h Jurgen Schmidhuber's physics PhD advisor ). Dr. Martin continues to colla
 borate with UC Berkeley on foundational AI research and has developed the t
 heory of heavy-tailed self-regularization (HTSR). He is the creator and lea
 d of the open-source WeightWatcher project\, which has over 175\,000 downlo
 ads\, and uses HTSR theory to assess the quality of neural network layers. 
 Throughout his career\, Dr. Martin has applied his expertise to develop and
  implement machine learning systems across various industries contributing 
 to projects at organizations including Roche\, France Telecom\, GoDaddy\, A
 ardvark (acquired by Google)\, eBay\, eHow\, Walmart\, GLG\, Barclays/BGI\,
  and BlackRock. Beyond his professional endeavors\, Dr. Martin offers scien
 tific consulting to the Page family office at the Anthropocene Institute\, 
 providing insights on nuclear and quantum technologies with a focus on addr
 essing climate change. His extensive experience spans over 15 years in comm
 ercial data science\, software engineering\, and machine learning/AI.\n\n-:
