CREATED:20250122T184651Z
DESCRIPTION:Adam has been shown to outperform gradient descent in training 
 large language transformers\, and by a larger margin than on other tasks\, 
 but it is unclear why this happens. We show that the heavy-tailed imbalance
  found in language modeling tasks leads to optimization difficulties. When 
 trained with gradient descent\, the loss on infrequent words decreases more
  slowly than the loss on frequent ones. As most samples come from infrequen
 t words\, the average loss decreases slowly. On the other hand\, Adam and s
 ign-based methods are less affected and improve predictions on all words. W
 e show empirically that this behavior persist through different architectur
 es and data types\, on language transformers\, vision CNNs\, and linear mod
 els. We formalize this observation for linear models and show that the type
  of imbalance found in text data leads to worse performance for gradient de
 scent than for sign descent.\n\nFrederik Kunstner is a postdoctoral fellow 
 at INRIA in Paris working on the intersection between machine learning and 
 optimization. His research focuses on identifying why methods that work bes
 t in practice outperform classical algorithms. Frederik’s paper on the EM a
 lgorithm received the best paper award at AISTATS 2021\, and his research h
 as been supported by an RBC Borealis Fellowship. He received his PhD at the
  University of British Columbia in 2024\, and his MSc and BSc from EPFL.\n\
