DESCRIPTION:As AI systems become embedded in Africa’s financial\, education
 al\, and public sectors\, governance must move beyond borrowed frameworks. 
 This session will explore how African nations can architect locally relevan
 t AI oversight ecosystems\, grounded in community values\, regional legal r
 ealities\, and cyber governance best practices. Drawing on examples from ac
 ross Africa\, I’ll examine the tension between innovation and regulation\, 
 the role of civil society in shaping AI standards\, and how we can co-creat
 e policy sandboxes that ensure AI works for and with African communities\, 
 not just on them.\n\nBetty W. Kyalo is a tech policy advocate and AI govern
 ance strategist with a footprint across Europe and Africa. With a backgroun
 d in cyber GRC\, program development\, and ethical tech implementation\, sh
 e bridges the gap between innovation and regulation. She has led initiative
 s that blend digital literacy\, public speaking\, and youth empowerment\, i
 ncluding landmark MOUs that enhance communication skills among tech profess
 ionals. Passionate about building African-led AI systems\, Betty is committ
 ed to shaping governance frameworks that are ethical\, inclusive\, and futu
 re-ready.\n\n-::~:~::~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:
 ~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~::~:~::-\nJoin with Google Meet: https://meet.google.com/r
 rj-sotc-yjt\nOr dial: (US) +1 575-448-4066 PIN: 318363099#\nMore phone numb
 ers: https://tel.meet/rrj-sotc-yjt?pin=2558023754953&hs=7\n\nLearn more abo
 ut Meet at: https://support.google.com/a/users/answer/9282720\n\nPlease do 
 not edit this section.\n-::~:~::~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~
 :~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~:~::~:~::-
